# Visual Studio components the windows-x64 build requires, and the files that
# prove each one is installed.
#
# Two columns, whitespace separated: a Visual Studio component id, and one path
# relative to the resolved MSVC toolset root -- <VS install>/VC/Tools/MSVC/<ver>
# -- that exists if and only if that component is installed. A component may
# have several rows. Lines starting with # are comments.
#
# This file is the single source of truth for both directions:
# scripts/provision-windows-toolchain.sh adds any component whose probes are
# missing, and scripts/verify-host-tooling.sh asserts every probe and names the
# component id when one is absent. Neither side keeps its own list, so they
# cannot drift apart -- the failure where a build provisions one set and checks
# another is not expressible here.
#
# WHY THESE AND NOT OTHERS
#
# Microsoft.VisualStudio.Component.VC.Tools.x86.x64
#   The C++ toolset. Chromium compiles with its own clang-cl but uses MSVC's
#   headers, libraries and some tools; docs/windows_build_instructions.md calls
#   for Microsoft.VisualStudio.Workload.NativeDesktop, whose Build Tools
#   equivalent is Microsoft.VisualStudio.Workload.VCTools, and this component is
#   that workload's C++ toolset. Both host arches are probed because
#   build/toolchain/win/win_toolchain_data.gni runs setup_toolchain.py for x86
#   AND x64 even when the target is x64 alone, and each run asserts
#   "cl.exe is not found in PATH" (build/toolchain/win/setup_toolchain.py:221).
#
# Microsoft.VisualStudio.Component.VC.ATL
#   ATL headers and libraries. base/win/atl_throw.h includes <atldef.h> and
#   base/BUILD.gn compiles base/win/atl_throw.cc into every Windows build;
#   base/win/atl.h pulls in eight more ATL headers from the same directory; and
#   third_party/dawn's DXC includes <atlbase.h> from
#   src/include/dxc/Support/WinIncludes.h. ATL ships only with Visual Studio,
#   never with the Windows SDK, and it is not part of the C++ toolset component,
#   which is why a runner with a complete compiler and SDK still fails at
#   "'atldef.h' file not found".
#
# NOT REQUIRED, DELIBERATELY
#
# Microsoft.VisualStudio.Component.VC.ATLMFC (MFC)
#   docs/windows_build_instructions.md asks for this one, because the IDE
#   checkbox it names is "MFC/ATL support" and MFC depends on ATL, so adding it
#   is how a human gets ATL through the installer UI. The build does not use
#   MFC: no file Chromium compiles includes an afx*.h header. remoting's
#   atlapp.h and atlcrack.h are WTL, vendored at third_party/wtl, and WTL needs
#   ATL rather than MFC. Installing MFC as well would be a larger download and
#   more disk on the target with the least of it, to satisfy a requirement that
#   is documented rather than real. If that judgement is ever wrong the symptom
#   is a named assertion here, in seconds, on a $0.16 probe run.
#
# Microsoft.VisualStudio.Component.VC.Tools.ARM64, ...VC.MFC.ARM64
#   docs/windows_build_instructions.md requires these only "if you want to
#   build for ARM64 Win32". build/args/windows-x64.gn sets target_cpu = "x64"
#   and there is no windows-arm64 target.
#
# The Windows SDK and its "Debugging Tools for Windows" feature are also
# required but are not Visual Studio components, so they are not listed here;
# scripts/provision-windows-toolchain.sh installs the Debuggers feature from the
# separately pinned build/WINDOWS_SDK_INSTALLER_* files.

Microsoft.VisualStudio.Component.VC.Tools.x86.x64	bin/Hostx64/x64/cl.exe
Microsoft.VisualStudio.Component.VC.Tools.x86.x64	bin/Hostx64/x86/cl.exe
Microsoft.VisualStudio.Component.VC.ATL	atlmfc/include/atldef.h
Microsoft.VisualStudio.Component.VC.ATL	atlmfc/include/atlbase.h
Microsoft.VisualStudio.Component.VC.ATL	atlmfc/lib/x64
